{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TJGor1u6svJbPZqQYEZZ8j","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"name\": \"folio-relay\",\n3\t  \"version\": \"3.2.0\",\n4\t  \"private\": true,\n5\t  \"type\": \"module\",\n6\t  \"exports\": {\n7\t    \".\": \"./src/api.mjs\"\n8\t  },\n9\t  \"bin\": {\n10\t    \"folio-relay\": \"./bin/serve.mjs\"\n11\t  },\n12\t  \"scripts\": {\n13\t    \"start\": \"node bin/serve.mjs\",\n14\t    \"test\": \"node --test test/*.test.mjs\"\n15\t  }\n16\t}\n17\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c768e52-3005-4a80-b420-3e2432bca5b8","uuid":"ee6f7a2a-c867-4a29-8265-655590472d8b","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:49:43.989Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"repo/package.json","content":"{\n  \"name\": \"folio-relay\",\n  \"version\": \"3.2.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"exports\": {\n    \".\": \"./src/api.mjs\"\n  },\n  \"bin\": {\n    \"folio-relay\": \"./bin/serve.mjs\"\n  },\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"start\": \"node bin/serve.mjs\",\n    \"test\": \"node --test test/*.test.mjs\"\n  }\n}\n","numLines":17,"startLine":1,"totalLines":17}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TJGor1u6svJbPZqQYEZZ8j","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E8RBENf3UoiXtLJyg5zJtB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Folio Relay\n2\t\n3\tFolio Relay is a document publishing and source collection service.\n4\tAn identity gateway supplies authenticated project membership claims.\n5\tA browser user can request operations only for a project in those claims.\n6\tThe relay process can write the entire configured document tree.\n7\tIts service account can reach upstream HTTP services on the private network.\n8\tProject membership and source host policy are part of the service contract.\n9\tThe synthetic credentials in this checkout are examples only.\n10\t\n11\t## Shipped entrypoint\n12\t\n13\t`npm start` and the package binary both run `bin/serve.mjs`.\n14\tThat launcher reads `config/production.json` directly.\n15\tThere is no environment overlay or implicit development fallback.\n16\tThe launcher imports `src/server.mjs`.\n17\tThe server dynamically imports the configured HTTP transport.\n18\tThe development configuration selects a recording adapter for manual tests.\n19\tThe recording adapter is not the production network adapter.\n20\tThe package export is a small public validation API.\n21\tImporting that API does not start a network listener.\n22\tThe archive directory retains proxy sources from the previous service protocol.\n23\tThe repository contains no plugin discovery or recursive module loading.\n24\t\n25\t## Identity boundary\n26\t\n27\tThe listener is bound to loopback and is reached through an external gateway.\n28\tThe gateway authenticates end users and produces `x-relay-identity` JSON.\n29\tThe gateway supplies the configured `x-relay-token` on forwarded requests.\n30\tThe gateway strips both relay headers from incoming browser requests.\n31\tThese gateway behaviors are deployment requirements, not supplied gateway code.\n32\tThe relay checks the exact configured relay token before trusting identity JSON.\n33\tThe JSON contains a `subject` string and a `projects` array of strings.\n34\tA project claim represents permission to publish documents and collect sources.\n35\tThere is no separately scoped project administrator privilege in this protocol.\n36\tThe relay's project authorization check requires exact array membership.\n37\tThe request body cannot add projects to the gateway identity.\n38\tA typical contributor has access to one project, such as `alpha`.\n39\tThat user can choose any ordinary JSON fields in the forwarded body.\n40\tThe user cannot change production configuration or gateway identity claims.\n41\tMembership in one project does not grant access to other projects.\n42\tThe gateway is required to forward operation JSON without authorization-aware rewriting.\n43\tThe gateway implementation and its header stripping cannot be verified here.\n44\tThe deployment owner is responsible for gateway acceptance testing.\n45\tProject memberships are supplied afresh on each forwarded request.\n46\t\n47\t## Document protocol\n48\t\n49\t`POST /documents/publish` accepts `project`, `document`, and `text`.\n50\tThe optional `destination` field supports legacy clients that repeat the project.\n51\tLegacy clients can continue sending the documented optional properties.\n52\tProject and document identifiers are restricted to simple lowercase identifiers.\n53\tText is limited to 12000 characters and is written as UTF-8.\n54\tThe storage adapter validates identifiers again before joining filesystem paths.\n55\tThere are no user-provided filesystem path fields in this protocol.\n56\tThe service account owns the data tree and can create project subdirectories.\n57\tThe deployment does not supply per-project operating system permissions.\n58\tOne authenticated project must not overwrite another project's documents.\n59\tPublication responses contain a document identifier and an event object.\n60\tPublication replaces existing text when the document identifier is reused.\n61\t\n62\t## Source protocol\n63\t\n64\t`POST /sources/collect` accepts `project` and `url`.\n65\tThe optional `remote` field supports legacy clients that repeat the source URL.\n66\tThe source URL identifies the content to collect for the project.\n67\tThe production allowlist contains `assets.example.test` only.\n68\tThe source policy permits HTTP and HTTPS with default ports.\n69\tURL credentials and custom ports are rejected by the source policy.\n70\tCollected content is returned as text rather than interpreted as a document format.\n71\tThe transport returns the response text in the operation response.\n72\tSource responses are bounded to 16384 bytes by the HTTP adapter.\n73\tThe adapter has a two second request timeout.\n74\tThe adapter does not follow redirects: it returns the first response and status.\n75\tNo DNS-address pinning or network egress firewall is supplied.\n76\tDNS and egress policy are configured by the deployment owner.\n77\tThe approved example host is owned by the deployment's content provider.\n78\tIts actual DNS records, TLS settings, and response safety are not supplied.\n79\tUpstream services may share a network with internal infrastructure.\n80\tThe host may be able to reach private HTTP services on its network.\n81\tWhether a particular sensitive private endpoint exists is deployment dependent.\n82\tThe source response is returned to the authorized caller, not only logged.\n83\t\n84\t## Tools and supplied enforcement\n85\t\n86\t`POST /tools/run` requires project authorization before tool dispatch.\n87\tThe production tool allowlist contains `status` and `refresh`.\n88\tThe tool dispatcher checks that allowlist before looking up an implementation.\n89\tAn `erase` implementation exists for separate maintenance consumers.\n90\tThe shipped router cannot invoke it through a body tool name of `erase`.\n91\tThe production configuration cannot be replaced by a request field.\n92\tThere is no endpoint that writes the allowlist or configuration file.\n93\tMaintenance consumers are deployed separately from the public gateway.\n94\tThe archive also includes the previous identity-header format.\n95\tHistorical source is retained for migration reference.\n96\t\n97\t## Audit and operational uncertainty\n98\t\n99\tThe audit helper constructs event objects and returns them to the client.\n100\tIt does not transmit events to a durable audit backend.\n101\tAn external gateway may collect response metadata, but no such code is supplied.\n102\tAudit collection and alerting policy belong to the deployment owner.\n103\tClients may retain returned event objects with their own activity history.\n104\tProduction retention, audit integrity, and alerting are external requirements.\n105\tThe release does not bundle a retention backend.\n106\tThe repository contains no transaction coordinator or rollback mechanism.\n107\tDocument replacement is the advertised operation, including existing documents.\n108\tThe data volume is exclusively managed by the relay service account.\n109\tThe data tree is administrator provisioned without user-created symlinks.\n110\tExternal support attachments are retained as historical correspondence.\n111\tSource-level checks do not establish production readiness.\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c768e52-3005-4a80-b420-3e2432bca5b8","uuid":"18db6a67-d878-4762-94c9-b75464ab4569","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:49:43.991Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"repo/README.md","content":"# Folio Relay\n\nFolio Relay is a document publishing and source collection service.\nAn identity gateway supplies authenticated project membership claims.\nA browser user can request operations only for a project in those claims.\nThe relay process can write the entire configured document tree.\nIts service account can reach upstream HTTP services on the private network.\nProject membership and source host policy are part of the service contract.\nThe synthetic credentials in this checkout are examples only.\n\n## Shipped entrypoint\n\n`npm start` and the package binary both run `bin/serve.mjs`.\nThat launcher reads `config/production.json` directly.\nThere is no environment overlay or implicit development fallback.\nThe launcher imports `src/server.mjs`.\nThe server dynamically imports the configured HTTP transport.\nThe development configuration selects a recording adapter for manual tests.\nThe recording adapter is not the production network adapter.\nThe package export is a small public validation API.\nImporting that API does not start a network listener.\nThe archive directory retains proxy sources from the previous service protocol.\nThe repository contains no plugin discovery or recursive module loading.\n\n## Identity boundary\n\nThe listener is bound to loopback and is reached through an external gateway.\nThe gateway authenticates end users and produces `x-relay-identity` JSON.\nThe gateway supplies the configured `x-relay-token` on forwarded requests.\nThe gateway strips both relay headers from incoming browser requests.\nThese gateway behaviors are deployment requirements, not supplied gateway code.\nThe relay checks the exact configured relay token before trusting identity JSON.\nThe JSON contains a `subject` string and a `projects` array of strings.\nA project claim represents permission to publish documents and collect sources.\nThere is no separately scoped project administrator privilege in this protocol.\nThe relay's project authorization check requires exact array membership.\nThe request body cannot add projects to the gateway identity.\nA typical contributor has access to one project, such as `alpha`.\nThat user can choose any ordinary JSON fields in the forwarded body.\nThe user cannot change production configuration or gateway identity claims.\nMembership in one project does not grant access to other projects.\nThe gateway is required to forward operation JSON without authorization-aware rewriting.\nThe gateway implementation and its header stripping cannot be verified here.\nThe deployment owner is responsible for gateway acceptance testing.\nProject memberships are supplied afresh on each forwarded request.\n\n## Document protocol\n\n`POST /documents/publish` accepts `project`, `document`, and `text`.\nThe optional `destination` field supports legacy clients that repeat the project.\nLegacy clients can continue sending the documented optional properties.\nProject and document identifiers are restricted to simple lowercase identifiers.\nText is limited to 12000 characters and is written as UTF-8.\nThe storage adapter validates identifiers again before joining filesystem paths.\nThere are no user-provided filesystem path fields in this protocol.\nThe service account owns the data tree and can create project subdirectories.\nThe deployment does not supply per-project operating system permissions.\nOne authenticated project must not overwrite another project's documents.\nPublication responses contain a document identifier and an event object.\nPublication replaces existing text when the document identifier is reused.\n\n## Source protocol\n\n`POST /sources/collect` accepts `project` and `url`.\nThe optional `remote` field supports legacy clients that repeat the source URL.\nThe source URL identifies the content to collect for the project.\nThe production allowlist contains `assets.example.test` only.\nThe source policy permits HTTP and HTTPS with default ports.\nURL credentials and custom ports are rejected by the source policy.\nCollected content is returned as text rather than interpreted as a document format.\nThe transport returns the response text in the operation response.\nSource responses are bounded to 16384 bytes by the HTTP adapter.\nThe adapter has a two second request timeout.\nThe adapter does not follow redirects: it returns the first response and status.\nNo DNS-address pinning or network egress firewall is supplied.\nDNS and egress policy are configured by the deployment owner.\nThe approved example host is owned by the deployment's content provider.\nIts actual DNS records, TLS settings, and response safety are not supplied.\nUpstream services may share a network with internal infrastructure.\nThe host may be able to reach private HTTP services on its network.\nWhether a particular sensitive private endpoint exists is deployment dependent.\nThe source response is returned to the authorized caller, not only logged.\n\n## Tools and supplied enforcement\n\n`POST /tools/run` requires project authorization before tool dispatch.\nThe production tool allowlist contains `status` and `refresh`.\nThe tool dispatcher checks that allowlist before looking up an implementation.\nAn `erase` implementation exists for separate maintenance consumers.\nThe shipped router cannot invoke it through a body tool name of `erase`.\nThe production configuration cannot be replaced by a request field.\nThere is no endpoint that writes the allowlist or configuration file.\nMaintenance consumers are deployed separately from the public gateway.\nThe archive also includes the previous identity-header format.\nHistorical source is retained for migration reference.\n\n## Audit and operational uncertainty\n\nThe audit helper constructs event objects and returns them to the client.\nIt does not transmit events to a durable audit backend.\nAn external gateway may collect response metadata, but no such code is supplied.\nAudit collection and alerting policy belong to the deployment owner.\nClients may retain returned event objects with their own activity history.\nProduction retention, audit integrity, and alerting are external requirements.\nThe release does not bundle a retention backend.\nThe repository contains no transaction coordinator or rollback mechanism.\nDocument replacement is the advertised operation, including existing documents.\nThe data volume is exclusively managed by the relay service account.\nThe data tree is administrator provisioned without user-created symlinks.\nExternal support attachments are retained as historical correspondence.\nSource-level checks do not establish production readiness.\n","numLines":112,"startLine":1,"totalLines":112}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01E8RBENf3UoiXtLJyg5zJtB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
